          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nAccording to the General clinical reference on asthma (condition-asthma), asthma is a long-term condition in which the lining of the airways is inflamed. The muscle around the airways is also prone to tightening, which narrows the airways and limits airflow. This narrowing comes and goes and can often be reversed.\n\nThe retrieved material does not describe what happens step by step during a flare-up. It does not cover the role of mucus, how inflammation changes over the course of an attack, or why symptoms get worse at particular times. Those are reasonable questions to ask a clinician or to look up in a fuller reference.\n\nIf you or someone near you is having severe breathlessness, trouble speaking, or blue lips or fingernails, seek emergency care right away.",
